fix: redirect to `$$` ICEd and `$!` went to the empty filename

`$$` and `$!` are first-class readable values — `str s=$$; > $s` has
always worked — but redirect-target codegen is slot-based and both are
served by intrinsic codegen, with no `VarSlot` behind them.

Used directly as a target they failed two different ways:

  /bin/echo hi > $$   ICE: unresolved redirect target — $$ has no slot
  /bin/echo hi > $!   compiled, redirected to the EMPTY filename

`$$` was skipped by the `var_name == "$"` guard in
`init_positional_params` ("served by other mechanisms") — true in value
position, false here, so no slot existed for the lookup to find. `$!`
had no guard, got an empty slot, and took the silent empty-filename
path that `spill_scalar_redirect_targets` exists to close
(bug-redirect-nonsimple-target-empty class).

Fix uses the mechanism already there: `target_needs_spill` now names
the slotless specials, so they bind to a synthetic scalar via
`expr_to_value` exactly like `${x:-f}` / `$a[i]`. `> $$` now means what
`str s=$$; > $s` meant; `> "$$.log"` falls out free, since the
predicate already recurses into `Concat`/`DoubleQuoted`. Both backends.

Found while probing those guards ahead of the dead-code purge: a cold
branch is not a dead branch, and this one was reachable and wrong.

Specs cov.250–cov.252. Sweep 5223 passing, 0 failing (predicted +6).
Journal 340.

